%[text] # Filter Banks, Step by Step: DFT Bank, QMF, DWT, CWT and MFCC
%[text] Pragmatic DSP Course. Needs MATLAB with the Signal Processing Toolbox (fir1, upfirdn, pwelch, spectrogram, buffer). Run section by section with Ctrl+Enter, from top to bottom.
%[text] Every bank in this script is shown with the same flow, so you can compare them: input signal, analysis, subband signals, synthesis, output signal.
%%
%[text] ## 1. What is a filter bank?
%[text] A filter bank splits one signal into several frequency bands, lets you work on each band separately, and (usually) puts the bands back together.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ x\[n\] \\rightarrow H\_k(z) \\rightarrow \\downarrow M \\rightarrow \\mathrm{subbands} \\rightarrow \\uparrow M \\rightarrow G\_k(z) \\rightarrow \\sum \\rightarrow \\hat{x}\[n\] $
%[text] It is made of only four building blocks:
%[text] - Analysis filters $H\_k$: each one passes a different frequency band.
%[text] - Downsampler $\\downarrow M$: keeps every $M$-th sample. A narrow band does not need the full sampling rate.
%[text] - Upsampler $\\uparrow M$: inserts $M-1$ zeros between samples to restore the rate.
%[text] - Synthesis filters $G\_k$ and an adder: smooth each band and sum them into the output. \
%[text] Three design choices tell the famous banks apart: how the bands are laid out (equal width, octaves, or mel), how much you downsample, and whether the output can equal the input (perfect reconstruction).
%[text] The test signal used in every section has four events that are easy to recognise: a 250 Hz tone that never stops, a click at 0.08 s, a 1100 Hz burst around 0.20 s and a 3000 Hz burst around 0.38 s.
clear; close all; clc;
fs = 8000;   L = 4096;   t = (0:L-1)'/fs;
x  = 0.8*sin(2*pi*250*t) ...                                   % steady low tone
   + 0.6*sin(2*pi*1100*t) .* exp(-((t-0.20)/0.04).^2) ...      % mid burst
   + 0.5*sin(2*pi*3000*t) .* exp(-((t-0.38)/0.03).^2);         % high burst
x(641) = x(641) + 1.5;                                         % click at 0.08 s

figure; %[output:92e599b3]
subplot(2,1,1); plot(t, x); grid on; xlabel('Time (s)'); title('INPUT: the test signal'); %[output:92e599b3]
subplot(2,1,2); spectrogram(x, hann(128), 96, 256, fs, 'yaxis'); title('Its spectrogram'); %[output:92e599b3]
% sound(x, fs)       % remove the % to listen

%[text] Before building a bank, see what the two rate-changing blocks do to a 3000 Hz tone.
tone = sin(2*pi*3000*t);
down = tone(1:2:end);                          % downsample by 2: new rate 4000 Hz
up   = zeros(2*L, 1);   up(1:2:end) = tone;    % upsample by 2: new rate 16000 Hz

sigs  = {tone, down, up};
rates = [fs, fs/2, 2*fs];
names = {'3000 Hz tone at 8 kHz', 'Downsampled by 2: the tone folds to 1000 Hz (aliasing)', ...
         'Upsampled by 2: a copy appears at 5000 Hz (image)'};
figure; %[output:54e8b95c]
for i = 1:3 %[output:group:7d43fdb9]
    [Pxx, f] = pwelch(sigs{i}, hann(512), 256, 512, rates(i));
    subplot(3,1,i); plot(f, 10*log10(Pxx)); grid on; axis tight; title(names{i}); xlabel('Hz'); %[output:54e8b95c]
end %[output:group:7d43fdb9]

%[text] Remember these two effects. Downsampling folds frequencies (aliasing), and upsampling creates copies (images). The analysis filters limit the aliasing and the synthesis filters remove the images.
%%
%[text] ## 2. Uniform DFT filter bank
%[text] Idea: design ONE lowpass prototype $p\[n\]$ and slide it along the frequency axis. Multiplying by a complex exponential shifts a filter to the centre frequency $2\\pi k/M$:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ h\_k\[n\] = p\[n\] e^{j 2\\pi k n / M}, \\quad k = 0, 1, \\ldots, M-1 $
%[text] With $M = 8$ and a sampling rate of 8 kHz the channels are centred at 0, 1, 2, 3 and 4 kHz, and channels 5 to 7 are the negative-frequency mirrors of 3 to 1.
%[text] Step 1, the filters.
M = 8;                                   % number of channels
D = 4;                                   % downsampling factor
N = 65;                                  % prototype length
p = fir1(N-1, 0.138);                    % lowpass prototype, about one channel wide
n = (0:N-1)';
H = p(:) .* exp(1j*2*pi*n*(0:M-1)/M);    % column k+1 = prototype shifted to channel k

Hf = fftshift(fft(H, 2048), 1);   f = (-1024:1023)'*fs/2048;
figure; plot(f, 20*log10(abs(Hf) + eps)); grid on; ylim([-80 5]); %[output:6d972b43]
xlabel('Frequency (Hz)'); ylabel('dB'); title('The 8 analysis filters: one prototype, shifted'); %[output:6d972b43]

%[text] Step 2, analysis: filter with each $h\_k$ and keep every 4th sample. The subband picture shows which channel is active at which time. This is exactly what a spectrogram is.
Y = zeros(ceil((L+N-1)/D), M);
for k = 1:M
    Y(:,k) = upfirdn(x, H(:,k), 1, D);   % filter, then keep every D-th sample
end

tm = (0:size(Y,1)-1)*D/fs;
figure; imagesc(tm, 0:M/2, abs(Y(:,1:M/2+1)).'); axis xy; colorbar; %[output:6b6287b9]
xlabel('Time (s)'); ylabel('Channel k  (centre = k kHz)'); title('SUBBANDS: size of each channel signal'); %[output:6b6287b9]

%[text] Read it: channel 0 is always on (250 Hz tone), channel 1 lights up at 0.20 s (1100 Hz), channel 3 at 0.38 s (3000 Hz), and the click touches every channel at 0.08 s.
%[text] Step 3, synthesis: insert zeros, filter each channel with the same shifted prototype, and add.
q = conv(p, p);
G = H * D / (M*q(N));                    % synthesis filters = analysis filters, rescaled
xr = zeros((size(Y,1)-1)*D + N, 1);
for k = 1:M
    xr = xr + upfirdn(Y(:,k), G(:,k), D, 1);   % insert zeros, then filter
end
xr = real(xr(N : N+L-1));                % remove the delay of N-1 samples

figure; %[output:52a2f1dd]
subplot(3,1,1); plot(t, x);      grid on; title('INPUT'); %[output:52a2f1dd]
subplot(3,1,2); plot(t, xr);     grid on; title('OUTPUT of the DFT filter bank'); %[output:52a2f1dd]
subplot(3,1,3); plot(t, xr - x); grid on; title('Difference'); xlabel('Time (s)'); %[output:52a2f1dd]
fprintf('DFT bank: reconstruction error = %.1f dB\n', 20*log10(norm(xr - x)/norm(x))); %[output:9fd0fae6]

%[text] The error is small but not zero, because a windowed-sinc prototype only approximately meets the reconstruction condition. Notes for practice:
%[text] - The channel signals are complex, and we kept $M/D = 2$ times more numbers than the input has. This bank is oversampled.
%[text] - Fast implementation: because all filters come from one prototype, the whole analysis can be done with one windowed FFT per output time (polyphase plus FFT). That is how spectrum analyzers and OFDM receivers work.
%[text] - Use it when you want equal-width bands: spectrum analysis, channelizers, equalizers. \
%%
%[text] ## 3. QMF bank: two channels, critically sampled
%[text] Now the smallest useful bank: a low half and a high half, each downsampled by 2, so the number of samples stays the same. The highpass is the mirror image of the lowpass around a quarter of the sampling rate, made by flipping the sign of every other tap:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ h\_1\[n\] = (-1)^n h\_0\[n\] $
%[text] Downsampling causes aliasing in both branches. The synthesis filters are chosen so the two aliasing terms cancel each other:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ G\_0(z) = 2 H\_0(z), \\quad G\_1(z) = -2 H\_1(z) $
%[text] What is left is the transfer function $T(z) = H\_0^2(z) - H\_0^2(-z)$, which should be a pure delay. That is one condition on the lowpass:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ |H\_0(e^{j\\omega})|^2 + |H\_0(e^{j(\\omega-\\pi)})|^2 = 1 $
%[text] Step 1, the filters.
Nq = 32;                                 % filter length (must be even)
h0 = fir1(Nq-1, 0.526);                  % lowpass; the cutoff is tuned so the condition nearly holds
h1 = (-1).^(0:Nq-1) .* h0;               % mirror image: highpass
g0 = 2*h0;    g1 = -2*h1;                % synthesis filters that cancel the aliasing

[F0, w] = freqz(h0, 1, 1024);   F1 = freqz(h1, 1, 1024);
figure; plot(w/pi*fs/2, 20*log10(abs([F0 F1]) + eps)); grid on; ylim([-80 5]); %[output:79bb7414]
xlabel('Frequency (Hz)'); ylabel('dB'); legend('H_0 (low)', 'H_1 (high)'); title('QMF analysis filters'); %[output:79bb7414]

%[text] Step 2, analysis: two subband signals, each at half the rate.
v0 = upfirdn(x, h0, 1, 2);               % low band,  rate fs/2
v1 = upfirdn(x, h1, 1, 2);               % high band, rate fs/2

t2 = (0:numel(v0)-1)*2/fs;
figure; %[output:6f1c9e1d]
subplot(2,1,1); plot(t2, v0); grid on; title('SUBBAND low (0-2 kHz): tone, click, 1100 Hz burst'); %[output:6f1c9e1d]
subplot(2,1,2); plot(t2, v1); grid on; title('SUBBAND high (2-4 kHz): click and 3000 Hz burst'); xlabel('Time (s)'); %[output:6f1c9e1d]
fprintf('Input: %d samples.  Subbands: %d + %d samples.\n', L, numel(v0), numel(v1)); %[output:7c72956d]

%[text] Step 3, synthesis and check.
xr = upfirdn(v0, g0, 2, 1) + upfirdn(v1, g1, 2, 1);   % insert zeros, filter, add
xr = xr(Nq : Nq+L-1);                                  % remove the delay of Nq-1 samples

figure; %[output:57a357f8]
subplot(3,1,1); plot(t, x);      grid on; title('INPUT'); %[output:57a357f8]
subplot(3,1,2); plot(t, xr);     grid on; title('OUTPUT of the QMF bank'); %[output:57a357f8]
subplot(3,1,3); plot(t, xr - x); grid on; title('Difference'); xlabel('Time (s)'); %[output:57a357f8]

alias = conv(h1, g0) + conv(h0, g1);                   % aliasing term: should be zero
Tz    = (conv(h0, g0) + conv(h1, g1)) / 2;             % what the signal sees: should be one impulse
TdB   = 20*log10(abs(fft(Tz, 4096)));
fprintf('QMF: aliasing = %.1e, ripple of T = %.3f to %.3f dB, reconstruction error = %.1f dB\n', ... %[output:group:254c656b] %[output:091d1d3a]
        max(abs(alias)), min(TdB), max(TdB), 20*log10(norm(xr - x)/norm(x))); %[output:group:254c656b] %[output:091d1d3a]

%[text] The aliasing cancels exactly, for any lowpass. The small error that remains is the ripple of $T$. Change the cutoff from 0.526 to 0.5 and run this section again: the error grows a lot, because fir1 then puts its half-amplitude point at the band edge, while the condition needs amplitude 0.707 there.
%[text] Polyphase: the same subbands at half the cost. The even taps only ever meet the even samples and the odd taps only the odd samples, so we can downsample FIRST and run two short filters. Because $h\_1$ is $h\_0$ with the odd taps negated, the low and high bands are just the sum and the difference of the two branches.
e0 = h0(1:2:end);            e1 = h0(2:2:end);        % even taps, odd taps
xe = x(1:2:end);             xo = [0; x(2:2:L-2)];    % even samples, odd samples delayed by one
a  = filter(e0, 1, xe);      b  = filter(e1, 1, xo);  % two short filters at half the rate
fprintf('Polyphase vs direct: low band %.1e, high band %.1e\n', ... %[output:group:4598a34f] %[output:469d2f60]
        max(abs((a+b) - v0(1:L/2))), max(abs((a-b) - v1(1:L/2)))); %[output:group:4598a34f] %[output:469d2f60]

%[text] Where QMF is used: the G.722 wideband speech codec, the subband stages of MPEG audio and HE-AAC, aptX, and subband echo cancellers.
%%
%[text] ## 4. DWT: the two-channel bank, repeated on the low band
%[text] Two changes turn the QMF bank into the discrete wavelet transform:
%[text] - Use a filter pair that reconstructs exactly. The orthogonal pair below is db4 in MATLAB naming: the Daubechies wavelet with 4 vanishing moments and 8 taps (some books call the 4-tap filter D4, which MATLAB calls db2). Its highpass is the alternating flip $h\_1\[n\] = (-1)^n h\_0\[N-1-n\]$.
%[text] - Split the LOW band again, and again. Each level halves the band and the rate. \
%[text] First look at the wavelet itself. Repeating the lowpass at finer and finer rates converges to the scaling function $\\phi(t)$, and ending with the highpass gives the wavelet $\\psi(t)$. These are the familiar db4 shapes.
loR = [ 0.230377813308855  0.714846570552542  0.630880767929590 -0.027983769416984 ...
       -0.187034811718881  0.030841381835987  0.032883011666983 -0.010597401784997];   % db4 scaling filter (8 taps)
hiR = (-1).^(0:7) .* fliplr(loR);                 % db4 wavelet filter

jj = 7;   c = loR;
for i = 1:jj-2
    c = conv(c, upsample(loR, 2^i));              % lowpass repeated at finer rates
end
phi = conv(c, upsample(loR, 2^(jj-1))) * 2^(jj/2);
psi = conv(c, upsample(hiR, 2^(jj-1))) * 2^(jj/2);
Ns  = 7*(2^jj - 1) + 1;   tt = (0:Ns-1)/2^jj;     % support: 0 to 7
figure; %[output:093d05ef]
subplot(1,2,1); plot(tt, phi(1:Ns)); grid on; title('db4 scaling function \phi(t)'); xlabel('t'); %[output:093d05ef]
subplot(1,2,2); plot(tt, psi(1:Ns)); grid on; title('db4 wavelet \psi(t)');          xlabel('t'); %[output:093d05ef]

%[text] After 3 levels at 8 kHz the bands are: d1 = 2 to 4 kHz, d2 = 1 to 2 kHz, d3 = 0.5 to 1 kHz, a3 = 0 to 0.5 kHz. The letter d means detail (high band of a split) and a means approximation (the last low band).
w0f = fliplr(loR);                                % analysis lowpass
w1f = (-1).^(0:7) .* fliplr(w0f);                 % analysis highpass (alternating flip)

J = 3;
[A, Dt, len] = dwt_tree(x, w0f, w1f, J);          % analysis: A = a3, Dt{j} = dj

figure; %[output:0ecc1de5]
subplot(5,1,1); plot(t, x); grid on; title('INPUT'); %[output:0ecc1de5]
for j = 1:J
    tj = (0:numel(Dt{j})-1) * 2^j / fs;
    subplot(5,1,j+1); plot(tj, Dt{j}); grid on; %[output:0ecc1de5]
    title(sprintf('SUBBAND d%d: %g to %g Hz, %d coefficients', j, fs/2^(j+1), fs/2^j, numel(Dt{j})));
end
tj = (0:numel(A)-1) * 2^J / fs;
subplot(5,1,5); plot(tj, A); grid on; xlabel('Time (s)'); %[output:0ecc1de5]
title(sprintf('SUBBAND a%d: 0 to %g Hz, %d coefficients', J, fs/2^(J+1), numel(A))); %[output:0ecc1de5]

%[text] Read it: the 3000 Hz burst lives in d1, the 1100 Hz burst in d2, the steady tone in a3, and the click shows up at 0.08 s in every detail band. High bands have many coefficients (fine time detail) and low bands have few. Some energy leaks between neighbouring bands because an 8-tap filter cannot separate them sharply.
xr = idwt_tree(A, Dt, w0f, w1f, len);             % synthesis

figure; %[output:1ebf4828]
subplot(2,1,1); plot(t, x);  grid on; title('INPUT'); %[output:1ebf4828]
subplot(2,1,2); plot(t, xr); grid on; title('OUTPUT of the inverse DWT'); xlabel('Time (s)'); %[output:1ebf4828]
fprintf('DWT: max reconstruction error = %.1e, with %d coefficients for %d samples\n', ... %[output:group:494d902d] %[output:2f93436f]
        max(abs(xr - x)), numel(A) + sum(cellfun(@numel, Dt)), L); %[output:group:494d902d] %[output:2f93436f]

%[text] Perfect reconstruction, with only 8 taps. Now use it. Signals made of smooth pieces and jumps need only a few large DWT coefficients, while noise spreads thinly over all of them. So: transform, shrink the small detail coefficients to zero, transform back.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ \\lambda = \\hat{\\sigma} \\sqrt{2 \\ln L}, \\quad \\hat{\\sigma} = \\frac{\\mathrm{median}(|d\_1|)}{0.6745} $
u  = (0:L-1)'/L;
s  = (u > 0.15) - 1.5*(u > 0.35) + 2*(u > 0.6) - 1.2*(u > 0.8) ...
   + exp(-((u-0.5)/0.02).^2) + 0.8*exp(-((u-0.9)/0.01).^2);          % steps and bumps
rng(2);   sn = s + 0.5*randn(L, 1);                                  % noisy version

[An, Dn, lenN] = dwt_tree(sn, w0f, w1f, 5);
sigma = median(abs(Dn{1})) / 0.6745;              % noise level, estimated from the finest band
lam   = sigma * sqrt(2*log(L));                   % threshold
soft  = @(v) sign(v) .* max(abs(v) - lam, 0);
sd    = idwt_tree(An, cellfun(soft, Dn, 'UniformOutput', false), w0f, w1f, lenN);

snrdb = @(ref, y) 10*log10(sum(ref.^2) / sum((ref - y).^2));
figure; %[output:3dddb2f8]
subplot(3,1,1); plot(s);  grid on; title('Clean signal'); %[output:3dddb2f8]
subplot(3,1,2); plot(sn); grid on; title(sprintf('INPUT: noisy, SNR = %.1f dB', snrdb(s, sn))); %[output:3dddb2f8]
subplot(3,1,3); plot(sd); grid on; title(sprintf('OUTPUT: DWT-denoised, SNR = %.1f dB', snrdb(s, sd))); %[output:3dddb2f8]

%[text] Where the DWT is used: JPEG 2000 image compression, denoising of biomedical and sensor signals, detecting edges and transients. It is weak on rich tonal signals such as music, because those are not sparse in the wavelet domain.
%%
%[text] ## 5. CWT: is it a filter bank too?
%[text] Yes. The continuous wavelet transform is the same idea as the DWT with two differences: it uses many closely spaced scales instead of one per octave, and it does NOT downsample. So it is an analysis filter bank of bandpass filters whose bandwidth grows with centre frequency (constant Q). Each filter is a stretched copy of one mother wavelet:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ W(a, b) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{a}} \\sum\_n x\[n\] \\psi^{\*}\\left(\\frac{n - b}{a}\\right) $
%[text] It is highly redundant (60 filters below give 60 times more numbers than the input), so it is used for LOOKING at signals, not for compression. Here it is with a Morlet wavelet, computed by filtering in the frequency domain.
fc = logspace(log10(100), log10(3600), 60);       % 60 centre frequencies, log spaced
w0 = 6;                                           % Morlet parameter: sets the Q of every filter
X  = fft(x);    w = 2*pi*(0:L-1)'/L;              % frequency axis, rad/sample
W  = zeros(numel(fc), L);    Psi = zeros(L, numel(fc));
for i = 1:numel(fc)
    a        = w0 / (2*pi*fc(i)/fs);                          % scale, in samples
    Psi(:,i) = 2*exp(-0.5*(a*w - w0).^2) .* (w < pi);         % frequency response of filter i
    W(i,:)   = ifft(X .* Psi(:,i)).';                         % one bandpass-filtered signal per row
end

figure; %[output:79c8c6c9]
subplot(2,1,1); plot(w(1:L/2)/(2*pi)*fs, Psi(1:L/2, 1:6:end)); grid on; %[output:79c8c6c9]
xlabel('Frequency (Hz)'); title('The CWT filter bank (every 6th filter): wider at high frequency'); %[output:79c8c6c9]
subplot(2,1,2); imagesc(t, 1:numel(fc), abs(W)); axis xy; colorbar; %[output:79c8c6c9]
yticks(1:10:60); yticklabels(round(fc(1:10:60))); xlabel('Time (s)'); ylabel('Frequency (Hz)'); %[output:79c8c6c9]
title('OUTPUT: scalogram |W|'); %[output:79c8c6c9]

%[text] Read it: the tone is a horizontal line, the bursts are blobs at the right time and frequency, and the click is a vertical line that is sharp at high frequencies and smeared at low ones. With the Wavelet Toolbox, the one-line version is cwt(x, fs).
%[text] - DWT: scales in powers of 2, downsampled, no redundancy, invertible and fast. Use it to process, compress and denoise.
%[text] - CWT: any scales, not downsampled, very redundant. Use it to analyse and visualise. \
%%
%[text] ## 6. Mel filter bank and MFCC
%[text] The last bank is built for recognition, not reconstruction. The flow is: cut the signal into short frames, take the power spectrum of each frame, sum it through triangular filters spaced on the mel scale, take the logarithm, and apply a DCT. The mel scale imitates hearing: fine resolution at low frequencies and coarse at high frequencies.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ m = 2595 \\log\_{10}\\left(1 + \\frac{f}{700}\\right) $
%[text] Step 1, the filters.
Nw = 256;   hop = 128;   Nfft = 512;   nMel = 20;   nCep = 13;
fbin   = (0:Nfft/2)*fs/Nfft;                                  % FFT bin frequencies (Hz)
hz2mel = @(f) 2595*log10(1 + f/700);    mel2hz = @(m) 700*(10.^(m/2595) - 1);
edges  = mel2hz(linspace(0, hz2mel(fs/2), nMel+2));           % triangle corners, equally spaced in mel
Fb = zeros(nMel, Nfft/2+1);
for m = 1:nMel
    Fb(m,:) = max(0, min((fbin - edges(m))   / (edges(m+1) - edges(m)), ...
                         (edges(m+2) - fbin) / (edges(m+2) - edges(m+1))));
end
figure; plot(fbin, Fb.'); grid on; xlabel('Frequency (Hz)'); %[output:81b52c56]
title('The 20 mel filters: narrow at low frequency, wide at high frequency'); %[output:81b52c56]

%[text] Step 2, analysis: frames, power spectrum, mel energies, log, DCT.
frames = buffer(x, Nw, Nw-hop, 'nodelay') .* hamming(Nw);     % one frame per column
Pw     = abs(fft(frames, Nfft)).^2;    Pw = Pw(1:Nfft/2+1, :);% power spectrum of each frame
logmel = log(Fb*Pw + 1e-10);                                  % SUBBANDS: log energy in each mel band
Cdct   = cos(pi/nMel * (0:nCep-1)' * ((0:nMel-1) + 0.5));     % DCT matrix
mfcc   = Cdct * logmel;                                       % OUTPUT: 13 numbers per frame

tf = ((0:size(frames,2)-1)*hop + Nw/2) / fs;
figure; %[output:843080f3]
subplot(3,1,1); plot(t, x); grid on; xlim([0 t(end)]); title('INPUT'); %[output:843080f3]
subplot(3,1,2); imagesc(tf, 1:nMel, logmel); axis xy; ylabel('Mel band'); title('SUBBANDS: log mel energies'); %[output:843080f3]
subplot(3,1,3); imagesc(tf, 0:nCep-1, mfcc); axis xy; ylabel('Coefficient'); xlabel('Time (s)'); %[output:843080f3]
title('OUTPUT: MFCC features'); %[output:843080f3]
fprintf('MFCC: %d samples in, %d frames x %d coefficients out. There is no synthesis stage.\n', L, size(mfcc,2), nCep); %[output:9fe0aec2]

%[text] The output is a small table of features, 13 numbers per frame, which is what a speech or sound classifier takes as input. The mel bank keeps only energies (no phase) and merges 257 spectrum bins into 20 bands, so the signal cannot be rebuilt from it. The DCT at the end packs the shape of the spectrum into the first few coefficients. With the Audio Toolbox the one-line version is mfcc(x, fs).
%%
%[text] ## 7. Side by side
%[text] - Uniform DFT bank: equal-width bands, complex subbands, oversampled here, near-perfect reconstruction. For spectrum analysis and channelizers.
%[text] - QMF: two bands, critically sampled, aliasing cancelled exactly, small distortion left. For subband speech and audio coding.
%[text] - DWT: octave bands, critically sampled, perfect reconstruction. For compression, denoising and transients.
%[text] - CWT: many constant-Q bands, not downsampled, analysis only in practice. For visualising when and where things happen.
%[text] - Mel bank and MFCC: perceptual bands on the power spectrum, no synthesis. For features in recognition. \
%%
%[text] ## 8. Your turn
%[text] - DFT bank: change the prototype cutoff from 0.138 to 0.125, then set M to 16 with N = 129. How do the subband picture and the reconstruction error change?
%[text] - QMF: plot the reconstruction error for cutoffs from 0.48 to 0.56. Where is the minimum, and why is it not at 0.5?
%[text] - DWT: replace db4 by Haar (set loR to 1 and 1 divided by the square root of 2, and change 0:7 to 0:1 and the 7 in Ns to 1). Compare the subband plots and the denoising result.
%[text] - DWT: in the denoising cell, try 2, 5 and 8 levels. Why is there a best value?
%[text] - CWT: change the Morlet parameter from 6 to 3 and to 12. What happens to the click and to the tone in the scalogram?
%[text] - MFCC: load the speech clip with load mtlb, then set x to the first 3968 samples of mtlb, fs to Fs, L to 3968 and t to (0:L-1)'/fs. Run Section 6 again. Which mel bands are active during speech? \
%[text] Concept check:
%[text] - Which block of a filter bank causes aliasing, and which block causes images?
%[text] - Why can all the filters of a DFT bank be computed with one FFT?
%[text] - In the QMF bank, what is cancelled exactly and what is only approximately right?
%[text] - Why does the DWT keep the same number of samples as the input while the CWT does not?
%[text] - Why can you not rebuild a signal from its MFCCs? \
%[text] Answers: (1) The downsampler causes aliasing and the upsampler causes images. (2) Every filter is the same prototype multiplied by a complex exponential, and summing over those exponentials is a DFT. (3) Aliasing is cancelled exactly by the choice of synthesis filters. The remaining transfer function is only approximately a pure delay. (4) The DWT halves the rate at every split, so the count stays constant. The CWT keeps every filter output at the full rate. (5) The mel bank discards phase and merges many frequency bins into a few overlapping bands, so information is lost.
%%
%[text] ## Helper functions (keep at the end of the file)
function [a, d] = ana2(x, h0, h1)
% One analysis step: filter and keep every 2nd sample.
a = upfirdn(x, h0, 1, 2);
d = upfirdn(x, h1, 1, 2);
end

function x = syn2(a, d, h0, h1, Lout)
% One synthesis step for an orthogonal pair; removes the delay, returns Lout samples.
Nf = numel(h0);
r  = upfirdn(a, fliplr(h0), 2, 1) + upfirdn(d, fliplr(h1), 2, 1);
x  = r(Nf : Nf+Lout-1);
end

function [A, D, len] = dwt_tree(x, h0, h1, J)
% J-level DWT. A = last approximation, D{j} = detail of level j, len = lengths per level.
D = cell(1, J);   len = [numel(x), zeros(1, J)];   A = x;
for j = 1:J
    [A, D{j}] = ana2(A, h0, h1);
    len(j+1)  = numel(A);
end
end

function x = idwt_tree(A, D, h0, h1, len)
% Inverse of dwt_tree.
x = A;
for j = numel(D):-1:1
    x = syn2(x, D{j}, h0, h1, len(j));
end
end

